<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hambach Festival</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hambacher Fest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hambach Festival was a mass demonstration held from 27 to 30 May 1832 at Hambach Castle near Neustadt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-palatinate">Bavarian Palatinate</a>, where some 20,000 to 30,000 people called for German national unity, freedom of the press and popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The black, red and gold flag was prominently displayed. The German Confederation answered with the Six Articles and further repressive measures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hambach-festival.jpg" alt="Hambach Festival" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Erhard Joseph Brenzinger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hambacher_Fest_1832.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Palatinate on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> had been occupied by French Revolutionary troops in 1794 and incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> in 1801, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat it passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, whose people suffered high taxes and growing censorship. Student fraternities, the Burschenschaften, had carried the black, red and gold colours of the Lützow Free Corps since 1815, and though suppressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> of 1819, the democratic movement revived after the French July Revolution of 1830 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, whose emigrants were warmly received in Bavaria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In January 1832 journalists founded an association for freedom of the press and speech, which the state government banned almost at once. Because demonstrations were forbidden, its founders called a fair at Hambach Castle instead. Workmen, women, students and members of parliament came, with guests from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and a delegation of 17 to 20 Polish emigrants. Speakers including Philipp Jakob Siebenpfeiffer and Johann G. A. Wirth blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s weakness on its division into thirty-eight states, and Henry Brueggemann led the crowd in swearing the oath of the Swiss cantons as Schiller gives it in his Tell.</p><p>The meeting demanded liberty, civil and political rights, national unity and popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-alliance">Holy Alliance</a>, but it reached no agreement on action: members of the Burschenschaften called for open revolt and a provisional government, which the journalists rejected. The song Fürsten zum Land Hinaus! originated at the festival and later became the unofficial anthem of the 1848 revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Three of the four organisers fled the country, and Wirth, who stayed, was sentenced to two years in prison. On 28 June 1832 the Federal Convention tightened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> again, and in 1833 Bavarian troops dispersed attempts to hold the festival on its anniversary, the year about 50 insurgents also failed to start a revolution by storming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> guard house. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine">Heinrich Heine</a> called the festival a missed opportunity, yet it is counted a milestone as the first mark made by a republican movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its colours were adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> and are used on the modern German flag.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hambach Festival” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>the Hambach Festival</b> of 1832 the Six Articles and further measures tightened control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ludwig I, king from 1825, began as a liberal but became increasingly authoritarian, especially after the <b>Hambach Festival</b> of 1832 in the Bavarian Palatinate, a mass demonstration for German unity and freedom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It was governed by Coburg officials who were unpopular, and the region was touched by the radical democratic movement around the <b>Hambach Festival</b> of 1832.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lichtenberg">Principality of Lichtenberg</a></li></ul></section>
